  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Simplify $1-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the numerator of the second term using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n1-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}=1-\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the second term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} & =1-\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\cancel{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =1-(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
